Map — key-to-value lookup.

A map stores key-to-value associations: give it a key, get back the value. A phone book, mapping a name to a number. A cache, mapping a request to a result. A tally, mapping a word to a count. It's the workhorse for "look something up by its identifier", and after list, it's the collection you'll use most.

The basics first. You make a hash-map from string to Integer. You put "Ada" with thirty-six, and "Grace" with forty-five. Then you put "Ada" again with thirty-seven — and because keys are unique, that replaces the old value. Get of "Ada" now gives thirty-seven. Get of a key that isn't there gives null. And that's why get-or-default is often the safer read — get-or-default of a missing key, with zero, gives you zero instead of null. Contains-key tells you whether a key is present, size gives the count, and remove takes a key away. The keys form a set — each key is unique — so putting an existing key replaces its value, and getting a missing key returns null.

Now, some methods that make maps genuinely pleasant, because hand-written "look, maybe create, then update" code is where map bugs live. To count words, you use merge: merge of the word, with one, using Integer-sum. For a brand-new key it stores one; for an existing key, it adds one to the old value — no null-checking, no "if absent" branch. To group items under a key, you use compute-if-absent: compute-if-absent of the key, creating a new array-list only if the key is missing, and then add the item to it. And put-if-absent only sets a value if the key isn't already there. Merge and compute-if-absent replace the classic "if the map contains the key, else" dance. Learn them — they come up constantly, and in interviews.

A map isn't iterable directly; instead, you iterate one of its three views. The idiomatic way is to loop over the entry-set, where each entry gives you get-key and get-value together. There's also key-set, which is just the keys as a set; values, which is just the values as a collection; and a for-each method that takes a lambda of key and value. Reach for entry-set when you need both key and value — it's a single pass, versus looking up each value by key separately.

As for implementations: hash-map is the default, with no ordering, constant-time get and put on average, and it allows a single null key. Use it unless you need order. Linked-hash-map preserves insertion order, and can even be configured as an L-R-U cache. And tree-map keeps its keys sorted, at order log-n, with navigation methods like first-key, ceiling-key, and sub-map; it uses compare-to, or a comparator, on the keys.

Finally, the keys carry the same contract as before. Because a lookup hashes the key and then compares with equals, a hash-map key type must have correct equals and hash code — records and string are ideal. And never mutate a key after inserting it: change a field that feeds its hash code, and the entry becomes unreachable, lost in the wrong bucket. So prefer immutable keys. That contract — shared by hash-set and hash-map — gets its own close look in section nine. But next, a different shape of collection entirely: queue and deque, for processing elements in order.
